---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.png";
import { courseMeta, coursePrerequisite } from "../course-config";
import { siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="Two inks on near-black: a single walled corridor spanning the frame, a marker's earlier positions fading out behind it, and a hazard-striped barrier closing the far end"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="slop-chips course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    Removing choice does not remove strategy. It relocates it.
  </p>

  <p>
    HEX ARAM takes away four things at once: you do not choose your champion,
    you cannot retreat and reset at will, there is no map to rotate around, and
    you cannot decline the fight. The usual conclusion is that what remains is
    execution — that the mode is the Rift with the interesting parts removed.
    That conclusion is a category error, and correcting it takes twelve weeks.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every decision the mode removes at the point of <em>selection</em> reappears,
    harder, at the point of <em>interpretation</em>. You are handed a champion
    and must work out what it can be made to do for this team, against this
    enemy, with the augments you happen to be offered. That is not less
    strategic than drafting. It is strategy without the comfort of having
    chosen the problem.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>

  <p>
    You will spend the semester arguing about single games. Each week takes one
    decision the mode forces — the roll, the reroll, an augment tier, a first
    purchase, a fight nobody could avoid, a death that was worth taking — and
    asks under what conditions the usual answer stops being right.
  </p>

  <p>
    The reading is a mix of first-party Riot material on how the mode is
    configured and observational data on what players actually do with it. You
    will be expected to tell those two things apart, and to say which one a
    claim of yours rests on. The three pieces of assessment are written
    accounts of decisions: a single opening state, a game read at its turning
    point, and a full match argued end to end.
  </p>

  <p>
    What you leave with is a defensible method for deciding under constraint,
    and the ability to say why a population-level statistic is or is not
    evidence for the decision in front of you.
  </p>

  <h2>What is marked</h2>

  <p>
    All three pieces are marked on the same four criteria: <strong>quality of
    reasoning under uncertainty</strong> (40), <strong>identification of the
    real trade-off</strong> (30), <strong>use of evidence</strong> (20) and
    <strong>clarity of the account</strong> (10). The course marks the argument
    for a decision, never whether the decision won. A well-argued defence of a
    choice that lost the game earns full marks.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>

  <p>
    Postgraduate students in games and interactive systems, offered as
    <strong>SLOP8223</strong> and, at undergraduate level, as
    <strong>SLOP4223</strong>. The prerequisite is {coursePrerequisite}: you are
    assumed to already know how damage, itemisation, levelling and objectives
    work, and the course does not re-teach them.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is not a course in improving your rank, and it will not tell you which
    champion or augment to take. If what you want is the current strongest
    option, that information is free, current and on Hexdata, and this course
    will spend most of its time explaining why it is not the same thing as the
    right decision.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will be able to do</h2>

  <ul class="outcomes">
    {courseMeta.learningOutcomes.map((outcome) => <li>{outcome}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The twelve teaching weeks, in the order the game forces the decisions.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Schedule" href="/schedule/">
      <p>Every lecture and due date on one page, with the constraint each week is about.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Three pieces of written work at 25, 25 and 50, and the criteria all of them are marked on.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Policies" href="/policies/">
      <p>Late work, extensions, generative tools, and the rule about which patch you argue from.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>

  <p class="riot-notice">
    League of Legends is © Riot Games, Inc. This course is not endorsed by,
    affiliated with, or produced by Riot Games. Convener:{" "}
    <a href={withBase("/people/spike/")}>Spike</a>.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
  }

  .lede {
    margin-block: 0 1.5rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 1rem + 1.6vw, 2rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.2;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .outcomes {
    padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
  }

  .outcomes li {
    margin-block: 0.4rem;
  }

  .riot-notice {
    margin-block-start: 3rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
</style>
